<Base title="Authors & Editorial Team: AC to DC Converter" titleIsComplete description="Meet the organizational editorial byline behind AC to DC Converter and understand its authorship, software validation and correction responsibilities.">
 <section class="shell page-intro legal"><p class="eyebrow">AUTHORSHIP & ACCOUNTABILITY</p><h1>Authors and editorial team</h1><div class="prose">
 <h2>AC to DC Converter editorial</h2><p>This is the organizational byline used for the site's technical guides. It identifies the publishing project, rather than a named professional engineer. The public <a href="https://github.com/actodcconverter1-ai/actodcconverter">source repository</a> records changes to the calculators, content and website.</p>
 <h2>Publishing and review responsibilities</h2><p>The site owner maintains the project and its private publishing dashboard. Guides explain the selected circuit model, cite relevant sources and link to worked examples. Software checks cover formula behavior and numerical boundary cases. These responsibilities do not imply a separate review board, laboratory staff or independent engineering certification.</p>
 <h2>Experience and credentials</h2><p>No individual biography, professional license, accreditation, employer affiliation or first-hand laboratory experience is claimed. We do not use stock portraits as team photographs. The <a href="/editorial/">editorial standards</a> explain automated assistance, source selection and the difference between software validation and physical testing.</p>
 <h2>Ask a question or report an error</h2><p>Use the <a href="/contact/">private contact form</a> to reach the publisher. Include the page URL, input values with units, the result and a supporting reference when reporting a technical correction. See <a href="/about/">About us</a> for the project's purpose and history.</p>
 </div></section>
</Base>
